  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening the Women&apos;s Rights Movement in Africa - PMU (cont. of PSIA92)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">FEMNET, also known as the African Women&apos;s Development and Communication Network, is a feminist organisation established in 1988 to promote women&apos;s development across Africa. The organisation aims to strengthen the role and contribution of African Non Governmental Organisations (NGOs) and individual activists who focus on women&apos;s rights, gender equality, women&apos;s empowerment, and the participation of women in development processes at different levels across the continent.   As a co -applicant with FEMNET for a European Commission grant, Oxfam Great Britain (GB) will be supporting FEMNET in its work to achieve effectiveness and impact of regional and country organisations on women&apos;s rights in three countries: Rwanda, Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC) and Ethiopia. The focus will be on improved relationships and engagements between Civil Society Organisations (CSOs) and policy -makers, a stronger and more vibrant women&apos;s movement in Africa, and lastly on ensuring that women&apos;s rights in Africa are recognised in key policy and development frameworks. More specifically, Oxfam GB&apos;s collaboration with FEMNET will seek to:  i. Strengthen the effective functioning and operation of FEMNET&apos;s Secretariat.  ii. Improve the capacity of members in policy advocacy and influencing on women&apos;s rights and gender equality and enhance collaboration, networking and sharing of information among FEMNET members and Oxfam partners.  This is a regional multi -country programme involving Oxfam GB in Rwanda, DRC and Ethiopia. This specific project, however, represents the above -country Multi Country Programme (MCP) coordination work of the Programme Management Unit (PMU). It is a continuation of the previous PMU project PSIA92, which reaches its 5 -year limit before the current contract end date (Dec 2023, now that the European Union (EU) has granted a 1 -year no -cost extension) .</narrative>
  </description>
